      "content": "### The Gilster-Mary Lee Outbreak and Bronchiolitis Obliterans\n\nIn May 2000, Dr. Allen Parmet, an occupational medicine physician in Kansas City, encountered a medical anomaly that defied clinical precedent. Several young, non-smoking factory workers from the Gilster-Mary Lee microwave popcorn plant in Jasper, Missouri, presented with catastrophic respiratory collapse. Their symptoms resembled end-stage chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) or acute refractory asthma: persistent, hacking coughs, profound exertional dyspnea, and rapid, irreversible lung destruction. When pulmonary function testing and lung biopsies were performed, the diagnosis was chilling: *bronchiolitis obliterans*, an exceptionally rare, devastating pulmonary condition characterized by the severe inflammation, fibrosis, and total fibrotic scarring of the terminal bronchioles. The scar tissue permanently plugged the microscopic airways of the lungs, suffocating the victims from within. For several of these workers in their twenties and thirties, the only medical option was a double-lung transplant.\n\nDr. Parmet alerted the Missouri Department of Health and Senior Services, which in turn requested an urgent Health Hazard Evaluation from the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health (NIOSH). Under the clinical leadership of Dr. Kathleen Kreiss, NIOSH industrial hygienists and epidemiologists descended on the Jasper plant. NIOSH\u2019s findings were swift, definitive, and horrifying. Workers who mixed heated soybean oil, salt, and industrial butter flavoring in giant open vats were inhaling dense chemical vapors dominated by diacetyl (2,3-butanedione), a volatile alpha-diketone responsible for rich, buttery aroma and taste. The epidemiological dose-response curve uncovered by NIOSH was virtually unprecedented in modern industrial hygiene: the risk of catastrophic airway obstruction among flavor mixers was dozens of times higher than expected, and even workers stationed hundreds of feet away from the mixing vats suffered significant, measurable declines in lung capacity.",
