      "keyInsight": "When you buy a prescription pill at the pharmacy, you trust the bottle. You assume researchers tested it carefully and revealed every single danger. But what happens when the pill makers control both the recipe and the lab notebook? The pharmaceutical industry realized it could hide nasty side effects simply by designing trials that buried the truth. If a painkiller doubled heart attacks, company memos told salespeople to focus entirely on pain relief. The wild paradox is that the public believed rigorous testing protected them. In truth, clinical trials were frequently treated like glossy advertising brochures. Independent watchdogs were silenced, data was buried in file drawers, and dangerous drugs stayed on shelves until the bodies stacked too high to ignore.",
      "content": "Imagine going to a restaurant where the chef gets to grade his own hygiene inspections. If an inspector spots a cockroach, the chef simply throws away that page and hands you a flyer praising the soup. That exact system operated inside the pharmaceutical market for blockbuster drugs like Vioxx. The maker knew years in advance that the arthritis pill strained human hearts. Instead of fixing the formula or warning patients, executives launched aggressive PR campaigns to discredit critical scientists.\n\nInside corporate offices, researchers sliced and diced test results until the warning bells stopped ringing. They dropped patients who suffered heart complications from the final reports, calling them unrelated flukes. It was like erasing low test scores so a failing school looks like an Ivy League academy. Doctors across the nation received free pens, luxury dinners, and ghostwritten medical articles assuring them the drug was gentle as water.\n\nBy the time regulators finally pulled the plug, tens of thousands of people had suffered fatal heart attacks. The scandal pulled back the curtain on drug safety. When pharmaceutical giants fund the research, manage the data, and pay the reviewers, safety becomes an inconvenient obstacle to quarterly sales targets.",
