      "headline": "Daubert: The Court Ruling You Never Heard Of",
      "keyInsight": "Imagine a baseball umpire who suddenly decides to rewrite the laws of aerodynamics during the World Series. That is essentially what happened when the Supreme Court ruled on Daubert. The judges intended to weed out crackpot witnesses and fortune-tellers from the witness stand. They turned local trial judges into elite gatekeepers of scientific validity. Sounds reasonable, right? But here is the bizarre twist: judges have law degrees, not chemistry doctorates. Corporate defense lawyers quickly weaponized this gap. Whenever an injured person brought forward respected scientists, companies flooded the judge with technical objections. Suddenly, standard toxicology tools used by doctors every single day were declared too speculative for court. An innocent rule created to block junk science became the ultimate tool to shut out genuine victims.",
      "content": "Before the Daubert ruling, a jury listened to expert witnesses from both sides and weighed the arguments. Jurors had common sense and could spot a hired con artist. But under the new standard, judges held private pretrial hearings to decide whether scientific methods met strict academic checklists. Corporate defense teams realized they did not have to convince a jury anymore. They only had to confuse one overworked legal clerk with dense statistical jargon.\n\nThink of it like hiring a piano tuner to inspect the wiring on an airplane. The tuner is smart and means well, but aviation instruments are entirely outside their wheelhouse. In courtroom after courtroom, judges threw out legitimate animal studies and chemical analyses because corporate attorneys argued they were not absolute proof of human injury. Because you cannot test toxic poisons directly on humans, this ruled out almost every realistic form of evidence.\n\nAs a result, regular citizens poisoned by factory runoffs found the courthouse doors locked tight. Companies no longer needed to prove their chemicals were innocent. They simply had to show that science involves normal, healthy debates. By pointing at ordinary scientific uncertainty, corporate attorneys convinced judges to dismiss lawsuits before a jury ever heard a single word.",
